SM(488,{"n":"Introduction to 1 Peter, 2 Peter, and Jude","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Introduction to 1 Peter, 2 Peter, and Jude.mp3","d":2733.3,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.68,"And uh tonight, at least a big chunk, uh, and who knows with me how long I'll ramble on about it, but at least uh a major portion of tonight we're gonna be doing introduction."],[36.56,"So I'm gonna kind of combine some introductions rather than doing an introductory section."],[44.16,"We're gonna do an introduction to three epistles tonight."],[46.96,"As I mentioned, we're this is gonna be the first time in going through the whole Bible I've gone out of order, but for reasons that we're gonna talk about tonight, we're gonna be talking doing an introduction to 1 Peter and 2 Peter and Jude, even though Jude comes after 1 2nd and 3 John."],[66.6,"And we're also going to be doing that introduction in the order 1 Peter, Jude, 2 Peter, for reasons that will become a literal clearer at least as we go on as to why uh why we're doing this."],[82.2,"So back when um I did my general introduction to the general epistles and sort of a general inception of generality or something, um we mentioned that the general epistles, unlike St."],[98.68,"Paul's epistles, the general epistles are more diverse."],[102.58,"Because this is sort of the other. epistles we have"],[107.66,"And so we have these three, which we've received as 1 Peter, 2 Peter, and Jude."],[120.7,"All three of these for different reasons."],[126.94,"Most scholars you'll ask, even like conservative Protestant scholars, the majority of scholars think that neither 1st nor 2 Peter were written by St."],[141.18,"Peter, nor was St."],[143.42,"Jude's epistle written by St."],[145.18,"Jude. Now these are for different reasons."],[147.18,"And so part of this introduction is going to be looking at each of them. and assessing those reasons and whether they're valid or not"],[155.46,"Mostly we're going to see not."],[162.98,"So we'll sort of start with 1 Peter."],[168.9,"And 1 Peter is in terms of order, remember, we talked about how the general epistles are just sort of ordered by length."],[178.48,"So, and they put 1st and 2 Peter together and 1, 2nd, and 3rd John together."],[184.13,"So 1 Peter is a little longer than 1 John, and 1st John is much longer than Jude."],[191.91,"So that's why they're in the order they are, not because people suspected that was the order they were written or or anything similar."],[201.07,"It's just longest to shortest."],[203.47,"But 1 Peter of these three that we're going to talk about tonight is the best attested."],[210.43,"What do I mean by that?"],[211.87,"I mean this is the one that we have the earliest manuscripts of."],[216.67,"This is the one that we find on the early canon lists."],[221.95,"Uh it takes a long time before we get 2 Peter and Jude turning up in people's canon lists."],[228.99,"We don't really get quotes of 2 Peter and Jude."],[234.83,"Well, of 2 Peter until like the 2nd cent century.ury, middle of the 2nd century, and Jude until like the 3rd"],[242.71,"Now, given Jude is like a paragraph."],[246.57,"So the fact that nobody quotes that paragraph out of the New Testament until the 3rd century doesn't mean it was written in the 3rd century."],[253.29,"Nobody thinks that."],[254.65,"But it's just to say we don't have a lot of, St."],[258.19,"Paul, everybody's quoting, very early on when we read the early fathers."],[263.01,"Even at the end of the first and beginning of the second century, other Christian writings are quoting St."],[268.53,"Paul, they're quoting the Gospels, and we find some quotes from 1 Peter."],[273.26,"Unlike 2 Peter and Jude, which we don't find being quoted until uh later on."],[279.1,"And it's referred to as the Epistle of St."],[283.94,"Peter by most of the earliest fathers and Christian writers who we have who refer to it."],[290.9,"They don't refer to it as the first epistle of St."],[293.22,"Peter, they just refer to it as the Epistle of St."],[295.7,"Peter. This is going to be one of the things when we get to eventually talking about 2 Peter that we're going to talk about."],[310.02,"Because if anybody looks at the first pages here, the little introductory pages of the Orthodox Study Bible, uh I'm about to contradict one of them very badly."],[322.26,"But there is a tendency among particularly conservative Protestant scholars uh to mistake uh dating everything in the New Testament ridiculously early for conservativism or traditionalism."],[351.82,"What do I mean by that?"],[353.26,"Well, liberal Protestants, like our 19th-century German friends, one of the ways in which they tried to break apart the New Testament in a testimony about Jesus, as we've talked about in a bunch of these introductions, is by trying to say everything was written really late."],[369.6,"Everything's from the second century cent.ury or the third"],[372.6,"That's now mostly been disproven."],[374.7,"For example, it was very common at the beginning of the 20th century to for scholars to say that St."],[382.54,"John's Gospel was written in the late 2nd century, and might have even been Gnostic."],[388.06,"And then we found a fragment of it from 125 AD in Egypt."],[394.86,"So it was like, oops."],[398.22,"Because it very clearly wasn't written in Egypt, so it had to be written before that, and then have time to get copied and get all the way to Egypt, so must have been at the at what was at the end of the first century."],[409.92,"So a lot of that's been disproven."],[412.08,"But before those things were disproven by findinging copies of the text and that kind of th, there was this sort of reactionary response, to those liberal Protestant scholars by conservative Protestant scholars to say, well, no, we're gonna say this was written, you know, St."],[431.58,"John's Gospel was written in 55 AD."],[435.79,"Just pull it back as early as they could."],[438.89,"And there are also some other reasons, especially in Calvinist theology, why they wanted to have everything written before 70 AD."],[446.87,"So everything got pushed back really far."],[450.23,"Okay. Um, and I know that a lot of people who are gonna hear this recordinging. that's why I'm say this now, uh, are still gonna have that kind of mindset"],[465.48,"That if you say anything was written after 70 AD, you're a liberal."],[470.78,"Or you're, you know."],[472.14,"Um here's the thing."],[475.42,"I don't feel the need to be more traditional or conservative than the church fathers."],[482.66,"So if the church fathers say that St."],[487.22,"Mark wrote his gospel in 69 AD, after the death of St."],[493.78,"Peter, based on his testimony, I'm gonna go with that."],[498.76,"And if they say St."],[499.64,"Luke and St."],[500.2,"John wrote their gospels after that, I'm gonna go with that."],[500.76,"Irenaeus tells me that St"],[503.6,"St. John had his vision that's recorded in the book of Revelation in 95 A.D. I'm just gonna go with that."],[512.0,"I don't need to be more conservative than them."],[514.64,"Okay. So when I'm about to talk about the dating of these, uh for 1 Peter, I'm gonna take the quote unquote conservative date."],[530.56,"Second Peter, I'm not going to take the conservative date."],[537.25,"But I'm doing that because I don't care about being quote unquote conservative or being scared of being called a quote unquote liberal."],[546.33,"I take it based on what the fathers say about these texts or don't say about these texts."],[552.83,"Because again, I don't need to be more conservative than them."],[556.19,"And if I agree with them and you want to call me a liberal, okay."],[559.73,"Me and St."],[560.37,"Arenaeus will be liberals together off somewhere."],[564.13,"Um so I just want to say that first."],[567.73,"So I I I everything I'm going to talk about here in terms of dating and who wrote things and all that is not based on me trying to be a liberal or conservative or accepting second."],[578.53,"It's based on the testimony we have from the earliest church fathers who quote from and talk about these texts."],[587.09,"Um, that I'm gonna try and go with them"],[591.65,"So, as I mentioned, what we find is the people we have very early references to 1 Peter, they just call it the Epistle of St."],[600.93,"Peter. So, this text, we talked about how when canon lists were made in the ancient church, they didn't have this sort of abstract notion of there is a canon of the New Testament."],[616.45,"What books are in it?"],[618.21,"Let's argue about it."],[619.75,"But that it was a question of what are the texts that we have in our church?"],[626.39,"That when we gather for worship, when we gather to celebrate the Eucharist, what are the texts that we read?"],[634.59,"And then preach about what texts are those."],[637.85,"And so we talked about how with the four gospels there's this incredible unanimity already in the beginning of the first or the second century, beginning of the second century."],[649.37,"And regarding St."],[650.49,"Paul's epistles, there's this incredible, nearly universal agreement by around 100 AD, where they're circulating as a collection."],[660.67,"And then we talked about in the introduction to the general epistles, that agreement part starts falling apart a little bit."],[668.17,"Um, and that should totally be shocking."],[670.98,"Because we talked about there that sort of those those divisions, those rough divisions of the New Testament kind of correspond to the divisions of the Old Testament."],[682.16,"And if you look at like Judaism in the Second Temple period between 500 BC and the first century AD, everybody agreed the Torah."],[690.9,"Everybody was clear the Torah's in."],[693.38,"There's just about everybody was like, yes, the prophets."],[697.54,"Then you get to the writings, and every group had their little disagreements."],[704.04,"There were some of those texts that like everyone had."],[706.56,"Eyverone had the Psalms, pretty much everyone had Proverbs, but then when you get to like Esther and Daniel, they had a bunch of different versions of Daniel."],[717.74,"There was less unanimity."],[720.43,"And that unanimity sort of comes, it doesn't even really totally come in the church."],[724.69,"Different Orthodox churches, as we talked about, have slightly different collections of writings in the Old Testament."],[731.97,"So the same thing happens with the New Testament."],[736.61,"That early on in the late first in the second century, and even in the beginning of the third century, there's a they're they're not identical."],[746.23,"And we talked about how, and we'll talk about this more when we get there, but the book of Revelation wasn't accepted across the board, especially in the East until like the 6th century."],[755.67,"There were a lot of churches still not using it"],[758.07,"That's why we don't read it in church, because our lectionary had already formed before that."],[762.95,"So it didn't get sort of added in"],[768.67,"But uh the general epistles, by the time you get to the middle of the third century, there's a pretty broad agreement on what's in the New Testament."],[779.23,"Um, everything except for Revelation."],[782.35,"So, 1 Peter, as the Epistle of St."],[786.91,"Peter, we find pretty much across the board."],[791.41,"Everybody refers to the Epistle of St."],[794.53,"Peter. Uh, it's not just concentrated in and around Rome, though it's very significant there."],[802.85,"Um, also in Egypt, also in as, we're gonna see eventually, 1 Peter is actually addressed to a bunch of churches in Asia Minor."],[814.33,"What's now Turkey, it's attested to there."],[817.05,"So it's I mean, a broad swath of churches have it."],[821.61,"So as I mentioned, all of these three epistles we're gonna talk about tonight, most people today say were not written by the people whose names are on them."],[833.05,"So in the case of 1 Peter, the argument is especially bad."],[837.9,"I literally saw a lecture on YouTube where a scholar said, made this argument with a straight face, clearly not thinking about it very hard."],[847.02,"Uh, where he said, Well, look, St."],[850.54,"Peter was a fisherman uh in Galilee, so he was he was probably illiterate, so he couldn't have written this."],[862.98,"Does anybody see the obvious huge problem with this?"],[866.02,"They dictated letters."],[867.23,"They dictated letters."],[868.61,"St. Paul didn't sit down and write any of his epistles either."],[872.21,"He was completely literate, but that's not how it worked."],[876.5,"So there is even, and we'll talk about when we get there, at the very end of First Peter, he refers to Saint Sylvanus, Saint Syllawan, who is going to take the letter to its recipients."],[893.38,"Okay, so it even mentions someone else involved."],[894.98,"Who certainly could have been the one writing the Greek"],[901.48,"Okay, that's number one."],[902.92,"Number two, Saint Peter was a Galilean."],[907.16,"Okay. In Galilee, everybody spoke both Aramaic and Greek."],[912.84,"That's why it was called Galilee of the Gentiles."],[918.36,"And how can we be so certain that St."],[920.84,"Peter was one of these people who also knew Greek?"],[923.4,"His brother's name was Andrew."],[926.44,"His brother had a Greek name."],[933.14,"So that's that makes it pretty clear."],[938.11,"And the way we see within the text of the New Testament, him called both Petros and Cephas the Arabaic and Greek version of the same nickname."],[951.81,"Not his given name, but nickname."],[957.03,"So the idea that because he was probably not himself literate in Greek meant he couldn't be the author of the is like ridiculous for somebody who's studied the New Testament, like at all."],[972.76,"But people still say it."],[974.28,"People still say it."],[975.64,"Um so with that given, that there's no reason St."],[979.88,"right Peter couldn't have written it?"],[980.17,"The biggest evidence that St"],[987.73,"Peter actually did write it is actually in the content."],[992.21,"And one of the biggest things is the addressees, who it's written to."],[997.41,"It's not written to one particular church."],[1000.61,"It's written to the churches across several areas, several Roman provinces."],[1006.37,"It says right at the beginning in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia."],[1014.77,"All of these provinces are in Asia Minor."],[1017.01,"They're a big swath of central and especially North Central Asia Minor."],[1024.29,"And they're written to those churches."],[1025.65,"That's why it's a general epistle, not St"],[1027.61,"Peter's epistle to this church."],[1030.45,"But here's what's interesting about all those places."],[1033.57,"And notice also that it's addressed there, we'll talk about this more when we start the epistle, but the pilgrims of the dispersion, the diaspora."],[1041.89,"Pilgrims of the diaspora in those places."],[1046.53,"So between the year 8042 and 80 54, the Emperor Claudius, one of the things he did was he started an aggressive campaign of uh setting up uh Roman colonies in exactly those provinces."],[1066.21,"exactly those provinces. Okay."],[1072.97,"And we mentioned before how Roman colonies worked that um they were these would mostly be military veterans and then a few other people who were being awarded with glant land grants."],[1084.97,"That was sort of the retirement plan after you'd served your time in the Roman legions."],[1089.45,"You would be given land, to go and and work and and settle on."],[1094.79,"And the Romans would deliberately set up these colonies around the edges of the empire and in areas that hadn't been sort of fully Romanized culturally yet."],[1106.55,"And so the idea was um that by setting up these Roman settlements, these would grow into Roman cities, they'd help with trade routes, but they would also help with bring the local cultures more fully into the Roman Empire and the Roman way of life."],[1122.67,"We talked about, for example, Galatia as one of these, when we talked about St."],[1126.51,"Paul's Epistle to the Galatians, we talked about how, in addition to Romans and Greeks living in Galatia, there were also sort of the native people who still had this pre existing culture, pre existing language, pre existing worship, that they were still doing."],[1142.69,"So these were areas where Claudius was attempting to sort of strengthen, sort of the Roman cultural grip."],[1154.15,"So he set up all these colonies during those years."],[1157.67,"The other thing, Claudius did during the same period that we talked about way back when we were talking about St."],[1163.63,"Paul's epistle to the Romans is in 49 AD, right in the middle, toward the end of that period, he expelled all the Jews from Rome."],[1176.51,"Expelled all the Jews from Rome in 49."],[1178.59,"And we saw in the Acts of the Apostles, this is how St."],[1181.79,"Paul ended up meeting Saints Priscilla and Aquila in Corinth."],[1187.31,"Because they had been ejected from Rome."],[1191.47,"Peter begins this, St"],[1193.51,"So the way St."],[1197.27,"Peter is going to identify where he's writing from as being Rome."],[1202.79,"We'll get into that more as we get into the text."],[1205.99,"Because he does it in kind of a coded way."],[1208.55,"He's writing from Rome."],[1209.99,"Why would St."],[1210.87,"Peter in Rome be writing to a whole bunch of people in these new Roman colonies over in Asia Minor?"],[1222.39,"Because they were a bunch of Jewish Christians who were resettled there when they were expelled from Rome."],[1232.12,"So this is a good pointer to sometime in the late 50s, early 60s before his death A.D."],[1243.8,"right St. Peter writing to them."],[1245.73,"And St. Peter, we're going to see this epistle is talking about tribulation and persecution, that these people have faced and are facing."],[1254.53,"We're going to go straight from that expulsion of the Jews."],[1257.17,"It's not going to be that long until Claudius is gone and Nero is on the scene."],[1262.85,"He's going to end up killing St."],[1264.45,"Peter. So the contents here very much suggest this kind of situation toward the end of St."],[1273.06,"Peter's life. So not only could St."],[1277.14,"Peter have written it, but the contents fit very neatly into that part of St."],[1282.74,"Peter's, St. Peter's life."],[1286.7,"So for all of those reasons, this is a uh pretty pretty clear attachment."],[1297.71,"Like I said, you we unanimously and very early have this being referred to as the epistle, the letter of Saint Peter."],[1305.31,"There's no real reason to doubt it."],[1308.52,"And it's it's not like St."],[1310.92,"Paul, where we have a whole bunch of letters and you can compare them to each other."],[1316.54,"We don't have something else written by St."],[1318.3,"Peter where they could compare it and say, oh, this looks different."],[1321.58,"We'll get to 2 Peter here in a minute."],[1324.7,"But so 1 Peter, pretty much a lock."],[1329.92,"Unless you have some agenda where you don't want St."],[1332.64,"Peter to have written it for some reason."],[1333.68,"reason There's really no to doubt the testimony."],[1333.76,"This is St"],[1337.48,"The universal and very early testimony of the church."],[1341.24,"Peter's Epistle. It fits very nicely in there historically."],[1345.64,"Okay, so that's Epistle 1, intro."],[1349.56,"Now we're going to, and it's not going to be clear immediately why, but when we get to 2 Peter, it'll be clear why."],[1355.08,"We're going to talk about the Epistle of St."],[1357.96,"Jude, which is just called Jude, our Bibles."],[1360.84,"And like I said, is a longish paragraph."],[1367.0,"So this is another one of those where uh we don't have anything else written by Saint Jude to compare it to."],[1377.48,"And frankly, even though there's this vast swath of scholars who want to deny that it was written by Saint Jude, there's no real reason why."],[1389.78,"And here's what I mean by that."],[1391.54,"So Jude is a this is a shortening of the name Judas or Judah."],[1398.36,"It would be Judah in Aramaic or Hebrew and Judas in in Greek, who was uh Christ's brother."],[1408.45,"So he at the beginning of the epistle identifies himself as the disciple of Christ and the brother of James."],[1415.07,"Saint James. Who would have been his older brother?"],[1419.45,"We talked about how these are half-rothbers when we in the introduction to uh uh or not half brothers, uh stepbrothers in the um introduction to St."],[1430.61,"James Epistle. So that's how the author identifies himself."],[1435.81,"Okay. So we don't have anything else other than his name being mentioned in a list of Christ's family members at a couple points."],[1446.13,"Anything else in the Bible about him?"],[1450.53,"Again, his name is mentioned a couple times, and there are these general references to Christ's brothers."],[1457.09,"That's what we have."],[1458.51,"And we've we've got some early church traditions, chief among which is that he went to Rome."],[1468.15,"But that's about it."],[1470.76,"So when you have a bunch of scholars denying that a text was written by the person who the text says wrote it, but about whom we know pretty much nothing."],[1484.53,"It's sort of like saying the Iliad wasn't written by Homer."],[1488.21,"Like, you could do that, but why?"],[1491.91,"Like, there's it's it, you know."],[1496.55,"Um and this is especially important because, like, with St."],[1502.07,"James's epistle. So St."],[1505.08,"Jude's brother, St."],[1506.12,"St. With James, he's this prominent figure in the book of Acts."],[1506.48,"James. And we have these other texts from the second and third centuries that claim they were written by St."],[1519.56,"James. So he was clearly perceived as being someone with authority."],[1523.41,"So if you were gonna forge something, we could tell from those other texts, he's the kind of guy who you'd claim wrote it."],[1532.02,"Why would you claim Saint Jude wrote it?"],[1534.74,"We don't have anything else that claims to be written by St."],[1537.62,"Jude. At all."],[1540.76,"Like, he's not mentioned anywhere."],[1543.08,"So you really have nothing to gain by claiming it was written by him."],[1548.97,"I mean, that doesn't give it a ton of, you know."],[1549.85,"it a So the whole idea that he didn't write doesn't make whole lot of sense."],[1558.05,"Okay. And uh for reasons we're about to talk about with 2 Peter, that identification of him with Rome seems pretty good."],[1567.01,"And it's a long-ish paragraph."],[1568.93,"So I mean, there's not really enough there for us to try and lock it down historically."],[1574.13,"We don't know for sure who he wrote it to."],[1576.47,"We don't know."],[1577.12,"It's very short."],[1578.88,"Um now, as I mentioned, it's not one of those texts that everybody's talking about early on."],[1587.28,"Doesn't really get referenced until like origin."],[1591.68,"So we're talking mid to late third century."],[1594.56,"And doesn't really show up on a lot of the early canon lists"],[1601.0,"A lot of the places where people list here's the texts used used by our church."],[1607.45,"But again, based on how short it is, based on the fact that there's not a really good reason to claim it was written by St."],[1616.81,"Jude, of all people, if it goes back to the first century and was written by who said it did."],[1629.85,"Now, that said, we can't lock it down to any particular place in the first century."],[1635.61,"So it's somewhere, you know, in in the the uh late 60s or early 70s, maybe, but that's kind of a guess."],[1645.51,"Because we just we don't have a lot of surrounding information, and it does end up being a text that's read in the church."],[1652.89,"And is quoted and cited in the church."],[1657.05,"Okay, now we get to the potentially controversial part, which is 2 Peter."],[1663.53,"So um. I don't want to openly bash the Orthodox Study Bible too much."],[1674.09,"But I'm gonna have to a little."],[1676.65,"So the Orthodox Study Bible in their introduction to 2 Peter says, while the Orthodox Church is clear on the canonicity of 2 Peter, that is true."],[1691.38,"2 Peter is a text that is read in the church."],[1694.02,"It is canonical for us."],[1695.86,"Okay. But now notice the next thing."],[1698.98,"Some modern scholars consider the letter to be pseudonymous."],[1705.38,"So first of all, some modern scholars, we're gonna talk about that in a second, but notice, consider it to be pseudonymous."],[1712.66,"As if that means it's not canonical."],[1722.86,"Did the prophet Samuel write 2 Samuel?"],[1727.02,"I I don't think so, because he died in 1 Samuel 17."],[1735.38,"So uh the pro uh the prophet Baruch wrote down the prophecies of Jeremiah."],[1741.94,"That doesn't make the book of Jeremiah non canonical."],[1747.03,"So there's an assumption there right off the bat."],[1750.65,"That something can't be, you know, if 2 Peter wasn't written by the historical Saint Peter, written by."],[1760.11,"Again, we'll put that in parentheses."],[1762.41,"Then it can't be canonical."],[1764.41,"That's an assumption."],[1765.37,"There's no basis for that assumption."],[1768.5,"If we had a time machine tomorrow."],[1771.08,"If St. Peter appeared to us, where it said, I didn't actually write that."],[1776.92,"We would not remove it from the canon."],[1781.85,"If he showed up and said, I didn't write that, Sylvanus wrote it after I died."],[1785.79,"We wouldn't remove it from the canon."],[1787.31,"That wouldn't change its canonicity at all."],[1791.21,"So that's a false assumption."],[1793.58,"Secondly, some modern scholars, it's not some modern scholars."],[1799.42,"It's like all scholars."],[1802.3,"Not just modern ones."],[1804.54,"Okay. You're gonna have a hard time finding church fathers who say St."],[1809.42,"Peter wrote this."],[1809.9,"Especially early on, where none of them even mention it"],[1815.2,"That's and where they refer to 1 Peter as the epistle of St."],[1819.2,"Peter. Okay, so there's a problem there, too."],[1824.88,"They say that is written by someone else based on what Peter taught."],[1829.36,"So if it was written by someone else based on what St."],[1832.4,"Peter taught, like, say St."],[1834.24,"Mark's Gospel, then it is not canonical, according to whoever wrote this."],[1841.36,"Kinda problematic. So that's just not a correct statement."],[1847.25,"And then it says vaguely, however, internal evidence supports Peter's authorship."],[1853.25,"Be nice if they told us what that was"],[1857.45,"Right? First of all, what even could that be?"],[1867.11,"What sort of internal evidence could there be that would prove that he wrote it and it wasn't written by someone else based on his teaching?"],[1877.19,"Because that's a fine distinction."],[1881.64,"That's a pretty fine distinction."],[1885.16,"Well, you found such evidence in First Peter."],[1888.6,"Well, well, that did that did prove that St."],[1892.6,"Peter wrote it rather than someone else based on his teaching."],[1897.96,"In fact, if he dictated it to a scribe, is that someone else writing it based on his teaching?"],[1908.04,"I mean, one could argue that all of St."],[1909.8,"Paul's epistles were written by someone else based on his teaching."],[1916.46,"So that whole thing is very problematic."],[1918.78,"So I'm kind of setting aside, and I'm doing this in advance, just so nobody flips to that and says, hey, the Orthodox Study Bible says."],[1926.7,"Because again, I don't know who wrote that."],[1929.82,"I'm sure they had the best of intentions."],[1932.14,"But that is just completely inaccurate."],[1935.34,"Okay. So here's here's the thing with 2 Peter."],[1941.54,"So the first thing that pretty much everyone has noticed, and when I say everyone, again, I'm not talking about modern scholars, just I'm talking about in the ancient church."],[1952.9,"The first person we really find talking about 2 Peter is actually Eusebius, and Eusebius says most churches didn't accept it because they didn't think St."],[1960.98,"Peter wrote it."],[1962.34,"That's like the first big word we have from the ancient church on 2 Peter."],[1967.62,"Okay? Now, let me be clear it is canonical."],[1973.08,"None of this matters to nothing I'm about to talk about has any relevance toward whether or not St."],[1979.64,"Peter 2 Peter is canonical."],[1981.4,"It is canonical in the Orthodox Church."],[1983.72,"Because we read it."],[1984.2,"read We it in church."],[1984.44,"It is part of the New Testament"],[1986.64,"It simply is."],[1990.14,"Regardless of who we decide we think wrote it."],[1993.58,"Regardless of who we and who wrote it was not an issue completely in the ancient church."],[2000.72,"Because pretty much everybody accepted St."],[2003.04,"Paul's epistle to the Hebrews, but if you read the Church Fathers, a huge swath of the church fathers didn't think St."],[2008.32,"Paul wrote it."],[2010.48,"That didn't mean they thought it wasn't canonical."],[2014.29,"And usually they thought some associate of St."],[2016.45,"Paul wrote it based on his teaching."],[2019.43,"Like St. Barnabas or St."],[2021.59,"Apollos. Um So, so, yeah, so none of this."],[2026.85,"Okay, it is canonical"],[2028.33,"Nothing I'm gonna say."],[2029.21,"Let me be clear about that."],[2032.09,"We're just talking about this canonical epistle."],[2036.57,"When was it written and who did the writing?"],[2038.81,"That's all we're talking about."],[2040.62,"Um, so uh the first big issue you run into when you read 2 Peter in Greek is that it looks nothing like 1 Peter."],[2051.34,"Right now, if you don't think St."],[2053.5,"Peter wrote 1 Peter, who cares."],[2057.16,"But since I do very much think Saint Peter wrote, in the sense we've talked about with this secretary with the scribe, wrote 1 Peter, this does create a problem, and it's not a little different."],[2069.4,"different. It is radically"],[2074.56,"Not just like Greek style and syntax vocabulary."],[2078.96,"There's something like 37 words used in 2 Peter in Greek that are not used anywhere else in the New Testament."],[2088.1,"So it's not just that it's not like 1 Peter, it's not like anything."],[2092.66,"Okay? Except one thing that we're going to talk about here in a minute."],[2095.92,"So it's very different."],[2097.52,"Now you could say."],[2099.09,"First thing to think, first thing to ask here is well, maybe he just had a completely different scribe."],[2105.67,"Maybe he just had a completely different scribe who had a completely different style."],[2109.76,"And who knows?"],[2110.6,"Mabey, maybe the scribe was bilingual, St."],[2113.64,"Peter dictated it in Aramaic, the scribe translated it into Greek, and because his Greek was totally different, the two epistles came out looking totally different."],[2122.92,"That's a plausible theory."],[2125.96,"It's a plausible theory, even though we have some extreme differences."],[2131.88,"But now we get into some more nitty gritty."],[2138.6,"And why I talked about the Epistle of St."],[2143.08,"Jude first. So almost the entirety of the text of the Epistle of Saint Jude is incorporated into and as Second Peter chapter 2."],[2161.52,"Now, if you want to go with this, Saint Peter is dictating it to two different scribes theory."],[2170.32,"Why did he read the scribe the Epistle of Saint Jude?"],[2179.28,"And why did that scribe who was doing such a different job with the syntax match Saint Jude's Greek syntax?"],[2192.72,"That creates a big problem."],[2195.38,"This creates a big problem."],[2198.26,"Um part three."],[2203.5,"Uh there is some other stuff outside the Bible that Second Peter looks a lot like."],[2212.78,"Okay. So there is a subset of literature within the Apostolic Fathers and shortly thereafter that came out of Rome in the area around Rome."],[2225.58,"So like 1 Clement."],[2228.56,"Saint Clement's epistle."],[2230.16,"Saint Clement is actually mentioned in the New Testament."],[2233.6,"He was one of Saint Peter's disciples in Rome."],[2237.04,"Okay, we have his epistle to Corinth that's saved as first Clement."],[2242.08,"The Shepherd of Hermas was written in Rome and its environs. at the end of the first and beginning of the second century"],[2251.48,"And there's another text which seems to cite 2 Peter called the Apocalypse of Peter that shows up, those written, people think it was written by 140 AD."],[2269.56,"It was written in the early part of the second century."],[2273.88,"And the language in 2 Peter is very similar to the language in those other writings that come from Rome at the end of the first century."],[2287.61,"And we have this text from the area around Rome that's claiming to be written by Saint Peter."],[2290.89,"ing. That's the earliest th we have that seems to"],[2296.93,"It doesn't quote it really, but it's using the same kind of language that Second Peter used."],[2304.18,"So what does that add up to?"],[2307.47,"So we've got St."],[2308.43,"Jude's Epistle that gets incorporated that was written in Rome."],[2312.85,"And is operating there, circulating in the church in and around Rome."],[2317.49,"We've got this kind of language that we see in the church in late first century Rome."],[2323.35,"Being used by church leadership."],[2326.55,"What this adds up to, Occam's razor, is that most likely this is something that was written after St."],[2333.37,"Peter's death at the heads of Nero based on his teaching"],[2340.06,"Probably sometime around 80 AD."],[2345.51,"So very much in the first century."],[2348.37,"And accurately reflecting what St."],[2351.25,"Peter was preaching and teaching in Rome."],[2354.07,"But this makes sense."],[2355.11,"If it's someone after St."],[2356.23,"Peter's death wanting to codify that, that explains why they'd incorporate like St."],[2360.87,"Jude's epistle, because this is something that's circulating in the Roman church."],[2366.61,"And we know from the church fathers that this is what happened with the gospel of St."],[2371.41,"Mark. That after St."],[2373.99,"Peter was martyred, Saint Mark went to Egypt and he wrote down, he wanted to preserve Saint Peter's testimony about Christ."],[2384.03,"And so he wrote it down."],[2387.39,"And so since that's not a problem for me with St."],[2391.63,"Mark's Gospel, I don't see why it would be a problem with 2 Peter."],[2396.51,"We talked about how, way, way back in the long ago time in the introduction to St."],[2401.95,"Paul's epistles, we talked about how with St."],[2404.11,"Paul and his writings that uh authorship was sort of on a spectrum."],[2412.15,"That St. Paul didn't actually sit down and put pen to paper and write any of these."],[2417.45,"We know for a fact he says so."],[2418.81,"He tells us who the scribe was, like in Romans"],[2422.59,"And he'll say, See with what big letters I write in my own hand when he comes and signs his name at the end."],[2429.6,"So we know he isn't writing them, writing them."],[2432.32,"But we have those that are very close to him."],[2435.46,"Where he's sort of directly dictating them."],[2438.02,"Like Galatians, Romans."],[2440.67,"1st and 2nd Corinthians."],[2442.67,"Then we have some where St."],[2444.91,"Paul lists a co-author."],[2448.21,"It'll say Paul and Timothy to this church, like Colossians."],[2454.67,"Like Ephesians. So St."],[2456.85,"Paul's still the author."],[2458.23,"But he has a co-author."],[2459.35,"So it's not his sort of direct."],[2460.15,"sort of And then of at the other end that, we talked about St."],[2465.03,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, that seems to be somebody's written record, possibly St."],[2471.59,"Luke's, of a sermon or homily preached by St."],[2475.67,"Paul. And so that's sort of the least direct from him."],[2481.94,"So authored by St."],[2485.86,"Peter can likewise be along this spectrum."],[2490.16,"And if you if you read what the church fathers say regarding the way Saint Peter's authority stands behind St."],[2501.12,"Mark's gospel. Because St."],[2503.0,"Mark was not an eyewitness to most of the events in his gospel."],[2506.36,"But St. Peter was."],[2508.1,"How St. Peter's authority stands behind that."],[2511.38,"Even though the church fathers don't say that about 2 Peter, they actually give less authority to 2 Peter than that, because they don't mention it as much."],[2520.11,"Some of them outright didn't know it, weren't familiar with it."],[2524.03,"If we use that as an example, that doesn't remove St."],[2528.43,"Peter's authority from this epistle just because he's not the one who put pen to paper."],[2533.01,"Because no one did that."],[2534.98,"So it is in no way problematic for 2 Peter to have been written in 80 AD."],[2542.31,"In order to record St."],[2543.83,"Peter's teaching, in Rome by someone else."],[2548.86,"Because he wasn't physically standing there."],[2552.32,"Any more that it's problematic that a secretary wrote down, possibly in a different language, what he told them while he was physically standing there."],[2562.58,"It's not more removal from St."],[2566.98,"Peter. And I don't think there's another way to be honest with what we have, especially when it comes to the way St."],[2576.6,"Jude's epistle is just incorporated in there."],[2582.26,"I mean, I guess you could say St."],[2584.1,"Peter wrote it in the early 60s and then St."],[2586.14,"Jude plagiarized him."],[2589.42,"Which would be weird that he would just pull out a piece of 2 Peter and plagiarize it for some reason."],[2600.22,"That seems weirder to me."],[2602.31,"That seems an odder thing to place at St."],[2605.27,"Jude than what I'm suggesting."],[2609.17,"So I think that is."],[2610.93,"This is also not a hill I would die on."],[2613.99,"This is me just piecing together based on the evidence we have from the church fathers and everything."],[2619.88,"This is the the the trying to do justice to everything."],[2623.32,"This is the best way I can do that."],[2626.46,"Without some kind of special pleading or just ignoring something."],[2633.34,"So yes, that is that is my take on what's going on with 2 Peter."],[2637.34,"And once we get into 2 Peter, I think we'll see some of that."],[2642.3,"2 Peter starts talking about Tartarus."],[2644.54,"So the place Tartarus is mentioned."],[2646.62,"There are these allusions to like classic."],[2649.58,"It's written in sort of quasi-attic Greek."],[2653.02,"There are lots of classical allusions in it."],[2656.22,"So that alone makes the Greek very different."],[2659.6,"But fits very much with Rome and with a lot of the literature we see from the end of the first century among Roman Christians."],[2669.12,"So yeah, we'll see that as we go."],[2671.12,"So that's. the introduction to the next three epistles we're going to be talking about, not in order"],[2677.16,"But you can see why I kind of had to talk about St."],[2680.04,"Jude first, because of the weird the weird thing with 2 Peter 2 and Jude."],[2690.68,"So uh yeah, send your send your hate mail to uh Father Andrew Damek, care of uh Ancient Faith Radio when this airs and you get angry at me for saying that."],[2704.52,"Uh because he loves to hear from you."],[2706.92,"He reads everything you send, even if he doesn't respond to it all."],[2711.0,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2716.28,"whole. Father Stephen's email address iscounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[2723.6,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Plan: three epistles, out of order","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tonight mostly <b>introduction</b> - combining intros rather than a separate section</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro to <b>1 Peter, 2 Peter, Jude</b> - first time going <b>out of order</b> in the whole Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude comes after 1-3 John, but reasons will become clear</li><li class=\"d1\">order: <b>1 Peter, Jude, 2 Peter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">General epistles (see earlier intro) <b>more diverse</b> than Paul's</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Most scholars</b> - even conservative Protestants - say neither Peter epistle by <b>St. Peter</b>, nor Jude by <b>St. Jude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">different reasons for each; will assess whether valid</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Mostly we're going to see <b>not</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":162.98,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter: best attested","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">General epistles ordered by <b>length</b>, not date</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Peter a little longer than 1 John; 1 John much longer than Jude</li><li class=\"d1\">1+2 Peter paired, 1-3 John paired</li><li class=\"d0\">1 Peter = <b>best attested</b> of the three</li><li class=\"d1\">earliest <b>manuscripts</b>; on <b>early canon lists</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter and Jude slow to appear on canon lists</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter not quoted until <b>mid-2nd c.</b>; Jude not until <b>3rd c.</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jude is \"like a paragraph\" - not being quoted doesn't mean written in 3rd c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul and Gospels quoted very early (end 1st / start 2nd c.); some quotes of <b>1 Peter</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Early fathers call it <b>\"the Epistle of St. Peter\"</b> - not \"first\"; matters when we reach 2 Peter</li></ul>"},{"t":310.02,"lv":2,"h":"Against ridiculously early dating","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Will contradict the <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b> intro pages \"very badly\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Conservative Protestants mistake <b>dating everything ridiculously early</b> for conservatism</li><li class=\"d0\">Liberal Protestants (<b>19th c. Germans</b>) pushed everything late - 2nd/3rd c. - to break up the testimony about Jesus</li><li class=\"d1\">disproven: John's Gospel \"late 2nd c., maybe Gnostic\"</li><li class=\"d1\">then a <b>fragment from 125 AD in Egypt</b> - \"oops\"; must be end of 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Reactionary response: John's Gospel in <b>55 AD</b>, pull everything back</li><li class=\"d1\">also Calvinist reasons for wanting everything <b>before 70 AD</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many listeners think anything after 70 is \"liberal\"</li></ul>"},{"t":475.42,"lv":3,"h":"Going with the fathers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I don't feel the need to be <b>more traditional or conservative than the church fathers</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers say <b>Mark</b> wrote 69 AD after Peter's death, Luke and John after → go with that</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Irenaeus</b>: John's Revelation vision in <b>95 AD</b> → go with that</li><li class=\"d0\">1 Peter: take the \"conservative\" date; <b>2 Peter: not</b></li><li class=\"d1\">based on what the fathers say or don't say about the texts</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Me and <b>St. Irenaeus</b> will be liberals together off somewhere\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Dating/authorship from <b>testimony of earliest fathers</b>, not liberal/conservative posture</li></ul>"},{"t":591.65,"lv":2,"h":"Canon as what the church reads","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Early references to 1 Peter: just \"Epistle of St. Peter\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient canon lists: not an abstract \"canon\" debate</li><li class=\"d1\">question was <b>what texts do we read at worship</b> / Eucharist, and preach on</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Four Gospels</b>: incredible unanimity by start of 2nd c.</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul's epistles</b>: near-universal agreement by ~<b>100 AD</b>, circulating as collection</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>General epistles</b>: agreement frays - shouldn't shock: NT divisions parallel the <b>Old Testament divisions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Second Temple Judaism: <b>Torah</b> everyone agreed; <b>Prophets</b> nearly everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Writings</b>: disagreements - Psalms, Proverbs universal; Esther, Daniel varied (several versions of Daniel)</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox churches still differ slightly on OT writings</li><li class=\"d0\">NT same: not identical in 2nd / early 3rd c.; <b>Revelation</b> not accepted in East until ~<b>6th c.</b> - why not read in church (lectionary formed already)</li><li class=\"d0\">By mid-3rd c. broad agreement on everything but Revelation</li></ul>"},{"t":821.61,"lv":2,"h":"Did Peter write 1 Peter?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">1 Peter attested across the board - Rome, Egypt, and <b>Asia Minor</b> (the addressees)</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument against is \"especially bad\" - YouTube scholar: Peter a Galilean fisherman, <b>illiterate</b>, so couldn't write</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Does anybody see the obvious huge problem?\" - <b>they dictated letters</b>; Paul didn't write his either</li><li class=\"d1\">end of 1 Peter names <b>Sylvanus</b> carrying the letter - could be the one who wrote the Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter a <b>Galilean</b> - Aramaic and Greek both spoken (\"<b>Galilee of the Gentiles</b>\")</li><li class=\"d1\">brother <b>Andrew</b> has a Greek name</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Petros</i> and <i>Cephas</i> = Greek and Aramaic form of the same <b>nickname</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Ridiculous for somebody who's studied the New Testament, like at all\" - but still said</li></ul>"},{"t":987.73,"lv":3,"h":"Addressees: Asia Minor colonies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Strongest evidence for Peter is the <b>content</b>, esp. the addressees</li><li class=\"d0\">Not one church but several provinces: <b>Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, Bithynia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">big swath of central / north-central <b>Asia Minor</b> → why a general epistle</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Pilgrims of the dispersion</b> (diaspora)\" - more when we start the text</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>AD 42-54, Claudius</b>: aggressive campaign planting <b>Roman colonies</b> in exactly those provinces</li><li class=\"d1\">colonies = mostly <b>military veterans</b> given land - retirement plan</li><li class=\"d1\">set on edges of empire, areas not yet Romanized → bring local cultures into Roman way of life</li><li class=\"d1\">Galatia an example (natives with own language, worship alongside Romans and Greeks)</li></ul>"},{"t":1154.15,"lv":3,"h":"Claudius expels the Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same period: <b>49 AD</b> Claudius <b>expelled all the Jews from Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how Paul met <b>Priscilla and Aquila</b> in Corinth (Acts)</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter writes from <b>Rome</b> - coded, will see in the text</li><li class=\"d0\">Why write to Asia Minor colonies? <b>Jewish Christians resettled there</b> after expulsion</li><li class=\"d0\">Points to <b>late 50s / early 60s</b>, before Peter's death</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistle speaks of <b>tribulation and persecution</b> - Claudius gone, <b>Nero</b> on scene, who will kill Peter</li><li class=\"d0\">Not only could Peter have written it - contents <b>fit neatly</b> into that part of his life</li></ul>"},{"t":1297.71,"lv":3,"h":"Verdict on 1 Peter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Unanimous, very early: \"the letter of St. Peter\" - <b>no real reason to doubt</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Unlike Paul, no other Peter writing to compare style against</li><li class=\"d0\">1 Peter \"<b>pretty much a lock</b>\" unless some agenda against Peter's authorship</li><li class=\"d0\">Fits historically</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistle 1 intro done</li></ul>"},{"t":1349.56,"lv":2,"h":"Jude: who wrote it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Next <b>Epistle of St. Jude</b> - why first will be clear at 2 Peter; a \"longish paragraph\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing else by Jude to compare; scholars deny authorship - \"no real reason why\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jude</b> = shortening of <b>Judas / Judah</b> (Aramaic/Hebrew Judah, Greek Judas)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ's brother</b>; opens as disciple of Christ and <b>brother of James</b></li><li class=\"d1\">James would be older brother - <b>stepbrothers</b> (see James intro)</li><li class=\"d0\">Elsewhere: name in lists of Christ's family, general references to \"brothers\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Early tradition: he <b>went to Rome</b> - and little else</li></ul>"},{"t":1470.76,"lv":3,"h":"Why would anyone forge Jude?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Denying authorship of someone we know nothing about \"like saying the <b>Iliad</b> wasn't written by Homer\" - why?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>James</b> a prominent figure in Acts; 2nd-3rd c. texts <b>forged in James's name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if forging, you'd claim <b>James</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing else claims to be by Jude; he's not mentioned anywhere → <b>nothing to gain</b> by using his name</li><li class=\"d0\">Link to Rome fits - reasons from 2 Peter, coming up</li></ul>"},{"t":1578.88,"lv":3,"h":"Jude: attestation and date","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not talked about early; first referenced by <b>Origen</b> - mid to late <b>3rd c.</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not on many early canon lists</li><li class=\"d0\">Short, and no good reason to attribute to Jude → likely first century, by who it says</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't lock to a place: \"late <b>60s or early 70s</b>, maybe - a guess\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't know who it was written to</li><li class=\"d0\">Still read and quoted in the church</li></ul>"},{"t":1657.05,"lv":2,"h":"2 Peter: the Orthodox Study Bible intro","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Potentially controversial part\" - has to <b>bash the OSB a little</b></li><li class=\"d0\">OSB: church clear on <b>canonicity</b> of 2 Peter (true - read in church); \"<b>some modern scholars</b> consider it pseudonymous\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem 1: assumes pseudonymous = <b>not canonical</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did <b>Samuel</b> write 2 Samuel? He dies in <b>1 Samuel 17</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Baruch</b> wrote down Jeremiah - doesn't make Jeremiah non-canonical</li><li class=\"d1\">time machine: Peter says \"I didn't write that, <b>Sylvanus</b> did after I died\" → wouldn't remove it from canon</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem 2: not \"some modern scholars\" - <b>all</b> scholars, ancient too; few fathers say Peter wrote it, early ones don't mention it</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem 3: \"internal evidence supports Peter's authorship\" - what could that be?</li><li class=\"d1\">can't prove Peter vs. someone writing from his teaching - a scribe could be \"someone else\"; by that logic all Paul's epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">Sets it aside up front; \"completely inaccurate\" though well intended</li></ul>"},{"t":1935.34,"lv":2,"h":"2 Peter: ancient doubts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First father to talk about it: <b>Eusebius</b> - most churches didn't accept it because they didn't think Peter wrote it</li><li class=\"d0\">Be clear: <b>canonical</b> regardless - \"we read it in church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">who wrote it was not a canon issue: <b>Hebrews</b> accepted, yet huge swath of fathers doubted Paul wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">thought an associate wrote from his teaching - <b>Barnabas, Apollos</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Only question: <b>when written, who did the writing</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2040.62,"lv":2,"h":"2 Peter's Greek is nothing like 1 Peter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek of 2 Peter looks \"<b>nothing like 1 Peter</b>\" - radically different</li><li class=\"d1\">not just style and syntax: ~<b>37 words</b> used nowhere else in NT</li><li class=\"d1\">not like 1 Peter, \"not like anything\" - except one thing (coming)</li><li class=\"d0\">Option: <b>different scribe</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bilingual scribe; Peter dictated in <b>Aramaic</b>, scribe translated → different Greek</li><li class=\"d1\">\"plausible theory\" despite extreme differences</li></ul>"},{"t":2138.6,"lv":3,"h":"Jude inside 2 Peter 2","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why Jude first: almost <b>all of Jude</b> incorporated into <b>2 Peter 2</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two-scribes theory then asks:</li><li class=\"d1\">why would Peter read the scribe <b>Jude's epistle</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">why would that scribe, with such different syntax, <b>match Jude's Greek</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">\"This creates a big problem\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2198.26,"lv":3,"h":"Roman literature parallels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">2 Peter resembles non-biblical works from <b>Rome and environs</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Clement</b> - Clement named in NT, <b>Peter's disciple</b> in Rome; epistle to Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Shepherd of Hermas</b> - Rome, end 1st / start 2nd c.</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Apocalypse of Peter</b> - seems to cite 2 Peter; written ~<b>140 AD</b>, early 2nd c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Language similar to Roman writings of late 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Adds up: Jude circulating in Rome + that Roman language in church leadership</li></ul>"},{"t":2326.55,"lv":2,"h":"Conclusion: written ~80 AD from Peter's teaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Occam's razor</b>: written <b>after Peter's death under Nero</b>, based on his teaching</li><li class=\"d1\">probably ~<b>80 AD</b> - \"very much in the first century\"</li><li class=\"d1\">accurately reflects what Peter preached in Rome</li><li class=\"d0\">Explains incorporating <b>Jude</b> - circulating in the Roman church</li><li class=\"d0\">Same as <b>St. Mark</b>: after Peter martyred, Mark went to Egypt and wrote down Peter's testimony</li><li class=\"d1\">no problem with Mark's Gospel → no problem with 2 Peter</li></ul>"},{"t":2396.51,"lv":3,"h":"Authorship as a spectrum","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul intro: authorship on a <b>spectrum</b>; Paul never put pen to paper</li><li class=\"d1\">scribe named (Romans); \"see with what big letters I write in my own hand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">closest: <b>Galatians, Romans, 1-2 Corinthians</b> - direct dictation</li><li class=\"d1\">co-author: \"Paul and Timothy\" - <b>Colossians, Ephesians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">least direct: <b>Hebrews</b> - written record (maybe <b>Luke</b>) of a Paul homily</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter's authorship can sit on that spectrum</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: Peter's authority stands behind <b>Mark</b> (not an eyewitness; Peter was)</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers give 2 Peter <b>less</b> weight - some didn't know it</li></ul>"},{"t":2534.98,"lv":3,"h":"No problem for 2 Peter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not problematic for it to be written in <b>80 AD in Rome</b> by someone else recording Peter's teaching</li><li class=\"d1\">no more removed than a <b>secretary</b> writing down, maybe in another language, what Peter said in person</li><li class=\"d0\">No other honest way given how Jude is incorporated</li><li class=\"d0\">Alternative: Peter wrote it early 60s and <b>Jude plagiarized</b> - weirder</li></ul>"},{"t":2610.93,"lv":3,"h":"Not a hill to die on","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Not a hill I would die on\" - piecing together fathers' evidence</li><li class=\"d1\">best way to do justice to everything, no <b>special pleading</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Preview of 2 Peter: mentions <b>Tartarus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">written in sort of <b>quasi-Attic Greek</b>, lots of <b>classical allusions</b> → very different Greek</li><li class=\"d1\">fits Rome and late 1st-c. Roman Christian literature</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Jude first: the 2 Peter 2 / Jude overlap</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Send your hate mail to <b>Father Andrew Damick</b>, care of Ancient Faith Radio\"</li></ul>"}]});
